<h1>5.4 Running Redis: memory, eviction, persistence and scaling</h1>
<p class="lead">Redis keeps everything in RAM. To run it in production you need to know what happens when memory is full, whether data survives a restart, and how to scale beyond one server.</p>

<div class="story" data-story="5.4"></div>


<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; Want to understand it deeply? Read the full explanation</summary>

<h2>When memory is full: eviction policies</h2>
<p>Redis's <code>maxmemory</code> setting caps memory use. When it is reached, <code>maxmemory-policy</code> decides what happens to the next write. You met these ideas in lesson 1.4:</p>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>noeviction</code></td><td>Writes fail with an error, nothing is removed</td><td>Redis used as a database or queue where losing keys is not acceptable</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>allkeys-lru</code></td><td>Remove the least recently used key, any key</td><td><b>The usual choice for a pure cache</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>allkeys-lfu</code></td><td>Remove the least frequently used key</td><td>Caches with stable popular items</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>volatile-lru</code> / <code>volatile-lfu</code></td><td>Like above, but only keys that have a TTL</td><td>Mixing cache keys (with TTL) and permanent keys in one Redis</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>volatile-ttl</code></td><td>Remove the key with the shortest remaining TTL</td><td>When near-expiry data is least valuable</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>allkeys-random</code> / <code>volatile-random</code></td><td>Remove a random key</td><td>Rarely the best, but cheap</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p class="muted">Redis's LRU and LFU are approximate: it samples a few keys and evicts the best candidate among them, which is much cheaper than tracking exact order.</p>

<h2>Persistence: does data survive a restart?</h2>
<div class="story mini" data-story="5.4-persist"></div>
<div class="grid grid-2">
    <div class="card">
        <b>RDB snapshots</b>
        <p class="muted">Redis periodically writes the whole dataset to a compact file. Fast restarts and easy backups, but you can lose the changes since the last snapshot.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <b>AOF (append-only file)</b>
        <p class="muted">Every write command is appended to a log, flushed every second (typical) or on every write. Loses at most about a second of data, but the file is larger and restarts are slower.</p>
    </div>
</div>
<p>For a <b>pure cache</b> you can run without persistence: after a restart the cache is cold and refills from the database. If Redis also holds data that exists nowhere else (sessions, leaderboards, queues), enable persistence.</p>

<h2>High availability and scaling</h2>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Setup</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Replication</b></td><td>Replicas keep a copy of the primary. They can serve reads and take over if the primary dies. Replication is asynchronous, so the newest writes can be lost on failover.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Sentinel</b></td><td>Monitors primary and replicas and promotes a replica automatically. Clients ask Sentinel where the current primary is.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Cluster</b></td><td>Splits data across several primaries. Every key maps to one of 16,384 <b>hash slots</b>, and each node owns some slots. Scales memory and throughput horizontally.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>In a cluster, a command that touches several keys only works if they are in the same slot. <b>Hash tags</b> force that: only the part in braces is hashed, so <code>{user:42}:cart</code> and <code>{user:42}:profile</code> land on the same node.</p>
<p>Most teams use a managed service instead of running this themselves: Redis Cloud (what your connection string points to), Azure Managed Redis, Amazon ElastiCache / MemoryDB, Google Memorystore. Redis-compatible alternatives include <b>Valkey</b> (open-source fork), Microsoft <b>Garnet</b> and <b>Dragonfly</b>; StackExchange.Redis works with all of them.</p>

</details>

