<h2>Experiments: do these in order</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li><b>Read the policy.</b> Find <code>maxmemory_policy</code>. A local Redis says <code>noeviction</code> by default; managed caches often default to a volatile or allkeys policy.</li>
    <li><b>Hit ratio at the server.</b> Use the Level 4 demos for a minute, then refresh here. <code>keyspace_hits</code> and <code>keyspace_misses</code> count every lookup on the server, a key metric to monitor (lesson 7.5).</li>
    <li><b>Key hygiene.</b> Look at the key list: keys from Levels 4 and 5, each with or without a TTL. Every cache key should have one.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What you should take away</h2>
<div class="callout tip">
    <strong>Key points</strong>
    Set <code>maxmemory</code> and an eviction policy (<code>allkeys-lru</code> for a pure cache). Decide whether you need persistence: not for a pure cache, yes if Redis holds data that exists nowhere else. Use replication for availability and cluster for scale, or a managed service. Watch memory, evictions and the hit ratio.
</div>
<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Common mistakes</strong>
    Using <code>noeviction</code> for a cache, so writes start failing when memory fills up. Big keys (a hash with millions of fields, a 50 MB string) that block the server and break even distribution in a cluster. Assuming replication means no data loss.
</div>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Level 5 complete</strong>
    You can now use Redis data types, build leaderboards and global rate limiters, broadcast messages, take safe locks, and reason about running Redis in production. Level 6 tackles the hardest part of caching: keeping cached data correct when the source changes.
</div>

